# nxupl

A bash tool to upload files to **Google Drive**, **Gofile**, **Pixeldrain**, or **Telegram** — works on regular Linux and Termux.

## Install & setup

### Locally
```bash
./setup.sh
```

### Or via curl one-liner
```bash
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/imnathanzero/nath/refs/heads/main/nxupl/setup.sh | bash
```

To pass flags through a pipe, use `bash -s --`:
```bash
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/imnathanzero/nath/refs/heads/main/nxupl/setup.sh | bash -s -- --no-sudo
```

Either way, `setup.sh` will:
- Detect Termux vs Linux
- Install missing dependencies (`curl`, `jq`, `rclone`, `python3`, and the Python packages `telethon` + `Pillow`)
- Install `nxupl` and `nxtgup.py` into **`~/.local/bin`**
- Create `~/.config/nxupl/nxupl.conf` from the example (permissions locked to your user only)

For the Python packages, it auto-adds pip's `--break-system-packages` flag **only** when you're on Ubuntu 24.04+ and *not* inside a virtual environment (that's the specific combo where pip otherwise refuses to install with an "externally managed environment" error). On any other distro, older Ubuntu, or inside a venv, it installs normally without that flag.

By default it **auto-installs** anything missing:
- On Linux, via `apt-get`, using `sudo` automatically if you're not root
- On Termux, via `pkg` (which never needs/uses sudo)

Pass **`--no-sudo`** (alias `--skip-sudo`) to skip all dependency installation and only check what's already present — `nxupl` still gets installed to `~/.local/bin` either way:

```bash
./setup.sh --no-sudo
```

If `~/.local/bin` isn't already on your `PATH`, the script will tell you and print the line to add to your shell config:
```bash
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
```

## Config

Edit `~/.config/nxupl/nxupl.conf`:

### Telegram setup
Telegram uploads use your **real account** (MTProto, via [Telethon](https://docs.telethon.dev)) rather than a bot, giving a file size limit of **~2GB** (4GB with Telegram Premium).

1. Go to https://my.telegram.org, log in, open **API development tools**, and create an app. Copy the **api_id** and **api_hash** into `TELEGRAM_API_ID` / `TELEGRAM_API_HASH`.
2. Set `TELEGRAM_USER_CHAT` to `me` (Saved Messages), a `@username`, or a numeric chat ID.
3. Run `nxupl telegram somefile.mkv` once. It'll interactively ask for your phone number, the login code Telegram texts/sends you, and your 2FA password if you have one. A session file is then saved to `~/.config/nxupl/` and you won't be prompted again.

**Security note:** the session file is equivalent to being logged into your Telegram account — anyone with that file can access your account without a password. `setup.sh` locks `~/.config/nxupl/` to `700` permissions; don't share or back up the session file insecurely.

#### Thumbnails
Every `nxupl telegram` upload interactively asks:
```
[?] Attach a custom thumbnail for this upload? [y/N]:
```
Say yes and give a path to an image (JPEG recommended, ≤200KB, ≤320x320px — Telegram's own limits). The script warns if your image doesn't fit those bounds but still lets you try. To skip the prompt when calling the Python helper directly:
- `--no-thumbnail` — skip and upload without one
- `--thumbnail /path/to/image.jpg` — use one without being asked

```bash
python3 ~/.local/bin/nxtgup.py video.mkv --thumbnail cover.jpg
```
(`nxupl telegram <file>` always uses the interactive prompt since that's its whole point — call `nxtgup.py` directly for non-interactive/scripted behavior.)

### Gofile / Pixeldrain
Work anonymously with no setup. Add `GOFILE_TOKEN` / `PIXELDRAIN_API_KEY` only if you want uploads tied to your account (so you can manage/delete them later).

### Google Drive setup (via rclone)
```bash
rclone config
# n) New remote
# name> gdrive
# Storage> drive (search "Google Drive")
# client_id / client_secret> leave blank to use rclone's default
# scope> 1 (full access) or 2 (read/write to files rclone creates)
# Follow the browser auth flow (rclone gives you a link + code for headless setups like Termux)
```
On Termux (no browser), choose "No" when asked to auto-open a browser, then run the given `rclone authorize "drive"` command on a machine that does have a browser, and paste the resulting token back in.

## Usage

```bash
nxupl gofile movie.mkv
nxupl pixeldrain photo.png
nxupl telegram big_movie.mkv
nxupl gdrive backup.zip --folder MyBackups
```

Each successful upload prints the resulting link and (if `xclip`/`wl-copy`/`pbcopy`/`termux-clipboard-set` is available) copies it to your clipboard. On Termux with `termux-api` installed (`pkg install termux-api` + the Termux:API app), you'll also get a notification.

## Files
| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `nxupl` | Main command, installed to `~/.local/bin/nxupl` |
| `nxtgup.py` | Telegram upload helper (Telethon), installed alongside `nxupl` |
| `setup.sh` | One-time setup: deps + install + config. Local or curl-piped. |
| `nxupl.conf.example` | Template for `~/.config/nxupl/nxupl.conf` |

## Notes
- Telegram uploads go through your own account, not a bot, so there's no 50MB bot cap — the practical ceiling is Telegram's own ~2GB (4GB Premium) per-file limit.
- Gofile/Pixeldrain anonymous uploads may be auto-deleted after a period of inactivity — check their current retention policies if that matters to you.
- `rclone link` requires the file/folder to be shareable; if your Drive is a shared drive with restricted sharing, you may need to open the file in Drive and share manually.
- `setup.sh` only automates dependency installation for apt-based Linux distros and Termux. On other distros, install `curl`, `jq`, `rclone`, `python3`, `telethon`, and `Pillow` manually, then run `setup.sh --no-sudo` to finish install/config.
- If you host these files for the curl one-liner, remember `nxupl.conf.example` will be publicly downloadable too — don't put real secrets in it, only the example is meant to be public.
